              <bucket>&quot;(MA-G) Möbel-Aktion Glas&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#e2c9a5'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Maurice Hamburger) Until his exile to Brazil, Maurice Hamburger was the representative (mandataire) of Isaac Hamburger in the early days of the German occupation of France.&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#e2c9a5'>
              <bucket>&quot;(MMannheimer) Paris&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#e2c9a5'>
              <bucket>&quot;(ORLevy) Paris&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#e2c9a5'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Raymond Poincare) Sammlung (avenue Raymond Poincaré)&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#e2c9a5'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Rosstein) Rosstein&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#e2c9a5'>
              <bucket>&quot;(SSkliartchik) Paris&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#e2c9a5'>
              <bucket>&quot;(VeuveADreyfus) St-Gilles/Bruxelles, Belgique&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#e2c9a5'>
              <bucket>&quot;(WP) Weil-Picard&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#eee5d8'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Aktion Berta) The Cassel property was plundered from an estate in Cannes and a house in Ruoms (Ardèche).  Some items were conveyed by train to Paris and then to Austrian repositories. Others transited via Marseille and were shipped to Germany. The Ruoms property went straight to Schloss Thurntal in Eastern Austria.&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#eee5d8'>
              <bucket>&quot;(BBLevy) Paris&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#eee5d8'>
              <bucket>&quot;(CCL) (Col. Colloredo)&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#eee5d8'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Deutsch) Deutsch&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#eee5d8'>
              <bucket>&quot;(EPS) Epstein, Delle b/Grenoble&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#eee5d8'>
              <bucket>&quot;(GBerg) Nice&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#eee5d8'>
              <bucket>&quot;(HB) Hamburger&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#eee5d8'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Jav) H. Javal&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#eee5d8'>
              <bucket>&quot;(JTokkie) Anvers, Belgique&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#eee5d8'>
              <bucket>&quot;(LAaronson) Cannes&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#eee5d8'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Louis Schick) Ville d&apos;Avray&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#eee5d8'>
              <bucket>&quot;(MA-MU (6 K)) Möbel-Aktion Münzen&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#eee5d8'>
              <bucket>&quot;(MEA) Mela&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#eee5d8'>
              <bucket>&quot;(MMeyer) Forest-Bruxelles, Belgique&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#eee5d8'>
              <bucket>&quot;(P. Lewin) Pierre Lewin&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#eee5d8'>
              <bucket>&quot;(RdWasserman) Bruxelles, Belgique&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#eee5d8'>
              <bucket>&quot;(RWeill) Paris&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#eee5d8'>
              <bucket>&quot;(STO) Paris&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#eee5d8'>
              <bucket>&quot;(VeuveLBlum) Ixelles-Bruxelles, Belgique-The German occupation emptied the residence of Mrs. Leon Blum on three occasions-31 August, 1 September, 2 September 1942.&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f0e6d2'>
              <bucket>&quot;(AContant) Ixelles-Bruxelles, Belgique&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f0e6d2'>
              <bucket>&quot;(ASiegfeld) Amsterdam, the Netherlands&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f0e6d2'>
              <bucket>&quot;(BRAUN) Mme. Robert Braun&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f0e6d2'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Cre) Cremieuse [Cremieux]&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f0e6d2'>
              <bucket>&quot;(EDreyfus) Paris&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f0e6d2'>
              <bucket>&quot;(FRE) Frenkel-Reder (Belgium)--The FRE collection represents a small percentage of the Jacob Reder collection which was seized in Brussels in August 1943. Go to JReder for a full list of confiscated objects. Also, check the BN collection for a number of Reder objects.&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f0e6d2'>
              <bucket>&quot;(GS) Gunther Schiedlausky-ERR&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f0e6d2'>
              <bucket>&quot;(HStern) Anvers, Belgique-The belongings of Helene Stern and her family, including those of Frederic Stern, were stored at La Continentale Menkès, chaussée d&apos;Anvers in Brussels, from which ERR agents removed them in a series of extractions. The removals began on 24 February 1942 and ended on November 2, 1943.&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f0e6d2'>
              <bucket>&quot;(JLantz) Paris-See Maurice Lantz&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f0e6d2'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Kram) Carl [Charles] Krämer&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f0e6d2'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Lib [Li.]) Libermann&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f0e6d2'>
              <bucket>&quot;(MA-B (2 A)) Möbel-Aktion Bilder&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f0e6d2'>
              <bucket>&quot;(MARG) (Jude) Margolinas&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f0e6d2'>
              <bucket>&quot;(MLBS) Möbel&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f0e6d2'>
              <bucket>&quot;(OPP) Oppenheimer--Although the ERR confiscated this collection under the name \&quot;Oppenheimer\&quot;, Elie Lantz recovered those OPP items that were repatriated to France by the Allies after the war.&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f0e6d2'>
              <bucket>&quot;(PRO) Berta Propper&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f0e6d2'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Rose) Rosenthal&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f0e6d2'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Sm) Seligmann-Alphandery-Pierre Seligmann-Alphandery was an “ingénieur diplômé de l’Ecole Polytechnique.”  Dr. Paetow and his team from the ERR paid a visit to the Seligmann-Alphandery apartment and retrieved a specific number of objects, mostly paintings and a tapestry which they brought back to the Jeu de Paume on 4 December 1942.  Then a second visit took place on 19 January 1943 during which the other items were confiscated—paintings, furniture, tapestry, enamel.&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f0e6d2'>
              <bucket>&quot;(v. Hell) unknown&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f0e6d2'>
              <bucket>&quot;(WJacobson) Paris&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f2ebdd'>
              <bucket>&quot;(AMLevy) Paris&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f2ebdd'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Belg. MA-MET) Belgisch Möbel-Aktion Metall (Belgium)&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f2ebdd'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Chapira) Paris&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f2ebdd'>
              <bucket>&quot;(DReder) Bruxelles, Belgique&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f2ebdd'>
              <bucket>&quot;(EW) Elizabeth Wildenstein&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f2ebdd'>
              <bucket>&quot;(GFistié) Ixelles-Bruxelles, Belgique-The building where Mr. Fistié lost his collection after being requisitioned, served as a depot for art works confiscated by the ERR.&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f2ebdd'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Hess) M. Raymond Hesse&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f2ebdd'>
              <bucket>&quot;(JEngel) Paris&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f2ebdd'>
              <bucket>&quot;(KALE) Kalmann-Levy&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f2ebdd'>
              <bucket>&quot;(LCDreyfus) Paris&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f2ebdd'>
              <bucket>&quot;(LWeill) Paris&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f2ebdd'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Madame Frey) Enghien-les-bains&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f2ebdd'>
              <bucket>&quot;(MFischer) Anvers, Belgique&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f2ebdd'>
              <bucket>&quot;(MUIR) Muir&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f2ebdd'>
              <bucket>&quot;(PJWeill) Paris&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f2ebdd'>
              <bucket>&quot;(RHaas) Bruxelles, Belgique-Mr. Haas&apos; collection was removed from his residence in Brussels on three occasions. On 21 April 1941, the Walon transport firm facilitated the removals; on 8 July 1942, the Société belge des extraits tannants facilitated the removals and on 18 July 1942, the Simon company aided in the removals of Haas property.&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f2ebdd'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Schloss) Adolphe Schloss-The Schloss collection was assembled with great care by Adolphe Schloss, a German Jew who settled in Paris in the late 19th century. In August 1939, the collection was transferred to a vault at the Chateau de Chambon (Corrèze) for safekeeping. On April 16, 1943, a combination of Nazi policemen and French officials from the CGQJ worked together to confiscate the Schloss collection and transfer it to Paris for processing. There, the collection was split into three parts--the largest going to the Linz Museum project. That part of the collection (262 paintings) was transferred to the Führerbau (Munich) on 24 November 1943 where it remained untouched until 29-30 April 1945 when local Munich citizens broke into the building housing the collection and hundreds of other works and stole everything. Nearly half of the Schloss paintings are still unrestituted.&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f2ebdd'>
              <bucket>&quot;(TEHerz) Forest-Bruxelles, Belgique-At the time of Mr. Herz&apos;s arrest, he was residing at 38, Avenue Charlotte, Anvers. The Gestapo arrested Mr. Herz on 3 September 1943. His belongings were removed therefrom on 5-6 September 1943.&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f2ebdd'>
              <bucket>&quot;(VRubia) Paris&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f4e9d8'>
              <bucket>&quot;(AGoldet) Paris&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f4e9d8'>
              <bucket>&quot;(AWeyl) Paris&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f4e9d8'>
              <bucket>&quot;(C.St) Caroline Stern&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f4e9d8'>
              <bucket>&quot;(D. W. Mod.) David-Weill&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f4e9d8'>
              <bucket>&quot;(EHahn) Paris&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f4e9d8'>
              <bucket>&quot;(FRT) louvre depot&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f4e9d8'>
              <bucket>&quot;(HA) Hugo Daniel Andriesse (Belgium)--The Andriesse collection was dropped off for safety reasons at the Musées Royaux d&apos;art et d&apos;histoire in Brussels on 12 December 1939 and 20 December 1939. The crates were removed by agents of the Devisenschutzkommando Belgien and transferred to the ERR in December 1941. From there, they were conveyed to the Jeu de Paume in Paris for processing.&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f4e9d8'>
              <bucket>&quot;(III) Seligmann collection seized by DSK in July 1940&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f4e9d8'>
              <bucket>&quot;(JPHirsch) Uccle/Bruxelles, Belgique&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f4e9d8'>
              <bucket>&quot;(L.H.) Levy Hermannos [Hermanos]&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f4e9d8'>
              <bucket>&quot;(LLambert) Paris&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f4e9d8'>
              <bucket>&quot;(MA-GT) Möbel-Aktion Gebrauchsteppiche&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f4e9d8'>
              <bucket>&quot;(MAY) Mayr [Blochmay/Bloch-May]&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f4e9d8'>
              <bucket>&quot;(MMDGoldschmidt) Bruxelles, Belgique&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f4e9d8'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Orloff-Justman) villa Seurat&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f4e9d8'>
              <bucket>&quot;(RB) R. Bottenwieser&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f4e9d8'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Rost) Erwin Rosenthal--The Rosenthal collection was among the first to be plundered by representatives of the ERR acting in concert with local Gestapo agents in Paris.  His apartment was ultimately emptied in the fall of 1942 during the so-called M-Aktion. Although it is impossible to date with certainty when the first thefts took place, some of Rosenthal&apos;s belongings were comingled with those of other collections which had been plundered in early 1941. As a result, many of Mr. Rosenthal&apos;s art objects are listed as \&quot;UNB\&quot;.  Some crates containing his property include the letters \&quot;EM\&quot; which are the initials of his street address while others are designated by the moniker assigned by the ERR to his collection (ROS or ROST).&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f4e9d8'>
              <bucket>&quot;(SSulzberger) Bruxelles-Susanne Sulzberger was an artist and art historian. Some of the objects looted from Sulzberger&apos;s home were found after the war in a Brussels depot on rue de Laeken.&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f4e9d8'>
              <bucket>&quot;(VeuveAronstein) Uccle-Bruxelles, Belgique-The US authorities dropped the claim on 28 June 1948.&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f4e9d8'>
              <bucket>&quot;(WTH) Paul Wertheimer&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f4ebdd'>
              <bucket>&quot;(AMorgoulieff) Neuilly sur Seine&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f4ebdd'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Belg. MA-OST) Belgisch Möbel-Aktion Ostasiatisches (Belgium)&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f4ebdd'>
              <bucket>&quot;(CHirsch) Paris&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f4ebdd'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Drey) Louis Dreyfuss&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f4ebdd'>
              <bucket>&quot;(F) Hans [Hermann] Fürstenberg&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f4ebdd'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Gim) Gimpel&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f4ebdd'>
              <bucket>&quot;(HGoldschmidt) Bruxelles, Belgique&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f4ebdd'>
              <bucket>&quot;(JEU) Jeunesse&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f4ebdd'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Kantorowitz) Paris&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f4ebdd'>
              <bucket>&quot;(LdL) Lévy de Leon&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f4ebdd'>
              <bucket>&quot;(LWolff) Boulogne&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f4ebdd'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Magitot-Laval) Andree Magitot-Laval&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f4ebdd'>
              <bucket>&quot;(MFu) Mayr-Fuld&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f4ebdd'>
              <bucket>&quot;(NBull) Schaerbeek-Bruxelles, Belgique--Mr. Bull&apos;s house was requisitioned on 10 December 1940. Mr. Bull&apos;s furnishings were stolen no later than July 1942. Some of it was sold off by the Brüsseler Treuhandgesellschaft.&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f4ebdd'>
              <bucket>&quot;(PLantz) Paris&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f4ebdd'>
              <bucket>&quot;(RHE) Rheims&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f4ebdd'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Schuster) Paris&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f4ebdd'>
              <bucket>&quot;(TFavory) Var&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f4ebdd'>
              <bucket>&quot;(VveEMendel) Bruxelles, Belgique&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f5e6cc'>
              <bucket>&quot;(AKantorovitch) Paris&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f5e6cc'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Bau) Bauer&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f5e6cc'>
              <bucket>&quot;(CBernheimdeVilliers) Paris&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f5e6cc'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Denn) Dennery&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f5e6cc'>
              <bucket>&quot;(EPorges) Paris&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f5e6cc'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Gal. Bernst) Galerie Bernstein (Paris)&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f5e6cc'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Harry Fuld) Berlin, Germany&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f5e6cc'>
              <bucket>&quot;(JArpels) Mougins&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f5e6cc'>
              <bucket>&quot;(JThannhauser) Paris&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f5e6cc'>
              <bucket>&quot;(L) Gisela Limberger-Goering assistant&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f5e6cc'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Lom) Lambert Lombard (Belgique)--It is quite likely that the name of the owner is not Lambert Lombard. Lambert Lombard (another name for Lambert Susterman) is the name of an artist who lived in the second half of the 16th century.  Hence, the painting classified as LOM 1 might be one that was produced by Lambert [Susterman] Lombard.&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f5e6cc'>
              <bucket>&quot;(MA-MK) Möbel-Aktion Modernes Kunstgewerbe (Glas)&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f5e6cc'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Me.P.) Leo Meyer&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f5e6cc'>
              <bucket>&quot;(MMeRHirsch) Ixelles-Bruxelles, Belgique&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f5e6cc'>
              <bucket>&quot;(OZ) Jeu de Paume&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f5e6cc'>
              <bucket>&quot;(RdSugny) Paris&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f5e6cc'>
              <bucket>&quot;(RUlmann) Paris&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f5e6cc'>
              <bucket>&quot;(Stern) Stern (Jacques?)&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
            <map to='#f5e6cc'>
              <bucket>&quot;(VeuveLBaur) Neuilly sur Seine&quot;</bucket>
            </map>
          </encoding>
        </style-rule>
      </style>
